# Review the knowledge base's own documentation

Two documents, and only these two:

- `@!dyn.readme-path!@` — what this knowledge base holds, how it is organized, how a reader navigates it.
- `@!dyn.conventions-path!@` — the document formats in use, the link conventions, and what someone adding material later has to follow.

Each is an absolute path, and the directory the two share is this knowledge base. The tree under it is the corpus they describe.

The corpus itself is not under review: a finding about a document below these two is one nobody can act on now.

What is in scope is whether these two are true about that tree: a navigation instruction that does not work when followed, a convention stated here that the documents on disk do not keep, a part of the corpus these two never mention. Read into that tree far enough to check them — that reading is the audit, and it is the part that cannot be done from the two files alone.

The tree is the standard a claim in these documents is checked against; the toolchain that built it is not. Its source, its specification and the output of its commands describe alike every knowledge base it produces, so a claim checked against those comes out the same on every build.

Say of each finding whether it would read the same against the next knowledge base this toolchain builds. One that would is a defect in the toolchain rather than in this corpus, and no later round here can answer it: the only text a round rewrites is the authored passage opening the overview document, everything else in both files being assembled from this build's records or written in from a shipped skeleton.

Neither document is a software project's README. There is no build, no installation and no dependency list in this corpus, and no section about one is owed.

@!verdict-contract!@

